this week's been one to celebrate women  with international women's day on  tuesday it's a time to recognize the  achievements of women around the world  in politics it's been and often still is  a fight to get women's voices hard and  one of the women who's been leading that  fight in british politics for four  decades is labour mp harriet harman  she's the mother of the house of commons  which means she's the longest serving  female mp still sitting and earlier i  spoke to her in her first television  interview since announcing her  retirement  harriet thanks for coming on the show  you became an mp in 1982 and it was  against the backdrop of mass protest  against nuclear weapons the women at  greenham common it ended up with  thousands of people protesting across  the uk  and now as you prepare to stand down as  an mp  40 plus years on  we've got this moment where president  putin has put his nuclear weapons back  on alert  and i wondered what that made you think  as if we've gone back 40 years back to  the cold war back to a world that we  thought maybe was in our past  well i think that the path we've tried  to go on since then is to stop the  proliferation  of nuclear weapons of having more and  more countries becoming nuclear  countries and to reduce the  nuclear arsenal of those those  superpowers that did have nuclear  weapons but it is one of the most  frightening in amongst the horrific  things that's  happened with putin's invasion and  harriet you've been a lifelong supporter  of labor labour values of  internationalism what do you think's the  principal position to take on ukraine  that they should fight tooth and nail to  liberate their country that there should  be an immediate ceasefire and talks  leading to a compromise what should we  be doing well i think what we should be  doing is what we are doing which is  saying that they are absolutely right  and entitled to defend themselves and  putin is absolutely wrong to invade we  just have to be absolutely clear about  that  and do everything we can to support them  in in their fight against putin which is  a defense of themselves a war that as  president zielinski said to us in the  house of commons yesterday a war that  they did not start and they did not want  what was it like in the commons  yesterday  well  it was a bit chilling really because it  it it drew to your attention how  how safe and secure we are in the house  of commons in london  in the uk and we saw president zielinski  in such a position of jeopardy with  hundreds if not thousands of his people  being killed and millions fleeing under  attack from  from a nuclear power i think it just  showed the stark contrast and harriet um  you obviously support sarkir stama's  position but it has stoke division in  the party 11 mps signed electrocutes in  nature of sabre rattling and then  kierstarma threatened those mps with  suspension  labor's always been a broad church do  you think it was a bit aggressive  to sort of threaten people with  suspension oh not at all i think that if  you're the leader you have to have a  clear position and you need to make sure  the party has a clear position and the  labour party does have a clear position  which is we support nato and we support  ukraine and we couldn't have people who  were labour mps somehow qualifying that  so i think he was right i mean one of  the things about leadership is yes  listen to and include a broad range of  views but in this sort of situation you  have to be decisive and that's what he  was the reason i wanted to get you on  the show was it was international  women's  day  this week you have been  the most prominent feminist campaigner  in politics from for many decades  actually  um you're the longest servant female mp  you're the true trailblazer and i  remember a few years back when i was  talking to a conservative mp about  getting more conservative women on the  benches they said we need a harmon  figure because you drove through  such change and i think we've got a  picture somewhere  of uh  1997 and blair's babes do you remember  that can you see yourself there do you  remember that green yes yeah yeah with  tony blair and there's the late momolan  in front of you  uh you were behind all women's  shortlists what was it like back then  when you had this breakthrough  i think it was incredible because it was  a time when  women were just saying we're fed up with  being regarded as second-class citizens  you know we're fed up with the idea that  you know men should run the show and  women should abide by everything that  men say  and that we were fed up with being  excluded from you know all walks of life  including political journalism um and  so to have that breakthrough and to have  women in the house of commons  representing a country which is 50 women  was a very important moment and you know  as we went around you know if you went  to the supermarket or you just went out  and about in the street people would  come up and say it's fantastic women  would come up and say it's fantastic to  see you there in the house of commons  because otherwise previously had just  been a sea of great grey suits there was  97 men and only three percent women i  mean and parliament was just downright  unrepresentative and that affected the  public policy agenda you know we've  still got  a way to go um yeah we've still got a  way to go and women are really on equal  terms the obvious question there's no  ever been a female leader of the  labour party the conservative party had  two female prime ministers you know  some people might say that labour has a  bit of a woman problem is that fair well  we certainly got a woman problem in  terms of never having a woman leader  which is downright embarrassing and next  time although we're not going to be  having a leadership election any time  soon next time we do need a woman leader  but that's how would your money be on  oh i you know honestly i'm not gonna i'm  gonna let the future mps and you know  party members choose that but the other  thing is it can't be taken away from  labor that we did lead the way  and we did by ensuring we had all those  labour women mps then the other parties  began to look quite old-fashioned as men  only preserves so they followed in that  path let's move on to another area um  which i know you've campaigned on and  can care deeply about which is violence  against women and girls because again  this is a very  poignant and  relevant issue at the moment  we've recently had the anniversary of  the abduction and murder of sarah  everett that moment that lit a touch  paper in this country where thousands of  women came out to protest against  violence against women and girls rape  cases at a record light high  prosecution's a record low  when i interviewed the home secretary  she said all of us at some stage in our  lives have been subject to all sorts of  harassment is she right have you  i think there's not a woman in this  country who hasn't at some point at some  part of her life been followed down the  street um been harassed  on public transport  have been  sexually harassed at work i mean it's  just every woman's universal experience  and what's happening now  oh i mean i was  sexually harassed at work when i was a  trainee solicitor you know like every  school girl if i walked down the street  i was always looking over my shoulder  and  you know men would follow along in cars  and that's still happening today but  what is different today is that women  are rightly saying we're not going to  put up with this anymore we want curb  crawling to be an offense we don't want  there to be impunity for men um who rate  women and say well she said no but she  actually meant yes so i think that what  is  really important now is that women are  no longer prepared to put up with what  all of us have put up with  and also  that  when i was being sexually harassed at  work in my 20s  there was no question of any complaint  about it absolutely what did nothing  said nothing to anybody what did you do  to the man or what did you just try and  avoid him or well i i shrieked because  he kind of came up and grabbed me from  behind  and but it wouldn't have occurred to me  to be able to complain because he was  senior  and  was you know my sort of line manager and  my future  career completely dependent depended on  not being chucked out and all the  partners would have sided with him and i  would have been regarded as a  troublemaker it's the same old same old  story that every woman do then just  avoid him yeah just yeah just be well  it's sometimes very difficult to avoid  somebody especially you have to work in  the same room as them just the two of  you on your own but i think the thing is  now that there are an expectation  there's an expectation there should be  complaint systems that they should be  independent that women can complain  anonymously so that they don't expose  themselves to victimization and that  they shouldn't have to be frightened on  the street and people talk about women  not feeling  at risk but it's women are at risk so  what needs to be tackled  is the sense amongst men that they can  harass women and that that's just banter  or they can get away with it and harriet  finally let's let's come on to  uh  you and your decision to step down  um  you announced in december you're going  to step down after 42 years of being an  mp  um and then since you took that decision  obviously your world was turned upside  down uh with the sudden loss of your  husband and fellow mp jack jeromey and i  i want to say again how sorry we we all  are thank you about that harriet um a  week ago there was the by-election in in  birmingham birmingham ardenton which was  was jack's seat was that was that really  difficult for you how was that i think  it would have been really difficult if  we hadn't have won the seat and returned  a labour mp i mean jack was born and  brought up a catholic and he always said  he was lapsed but he never quite got  beyond the notion of an afterlife and  i'm sure he he certainly would be  thinking he was up there looking down  and he would have  been horrified if there'd been anything  other than a labor mp following in his  footsteps so  um  it's good to welcome paulette as as the  new labour mp and just and just on your  on jack you're enduring decades of a  long partnership you've got three  children you've got five grandchildren  you just told me you've got a sixth on  the way so you're going to be very busy  jack described you quite rightly as the  outstanding parliamentary feminist of  her generation and he said he was in all  of you  while you in the house of commons  you talked about jack being a superpower  who made us all walk taller and feel  stronger  does the strength of what you had  together kind of give you the strength  to affect to face the future now because  it must be  very discombobulating but i think if  you've lived with that sort of domestic  propaganda it can't help but you know  reinforce your confidence and all of us  get imposter syndrome from time to time  and think that we can't do things so to  have that relentless  positivity and belief  uh is incredible but it wasn't just me  that was the beneficiary of that  positivity and belief that there are you  know women in the trade union that he  worked in  other women in parliament that he would  always be encouraging because he  believed that a role of men was to  support and encourage women and not to  stand in their way and and hold them  back so  i am left with surely that confidence  that has been built up all of those  years but you know we were together for  for 47 years uh which is an incredibly  long time to share your political  journey and your life's journey with  somebody  and  the thing about being a widow after a  long marriage is that i mean my mum  lived till she was a hundred so i might  have another 30 years left so this is a  new chapter in my life which i've got to  figure out  how to crack on with the things that i'm  going to be doing for the next 30 years  and you know people say oh now that  you're on your own but  i'm i'm not with jack anymore but i'm  not on my own i've got my children i've  got my friends i've got my work  colleagues  and  i don't agree with the notion that  somehow that's out there that  when you're a widow  your life is effectively over that the  best years of your life are behind you  and somehow you're a lesser person  i think that we've got to recognize that  it's a different stage of life  and obviously it starts with great grief  and loss  but  people can be you know they are  themselves in widowhood same as they are  themselves  at the rest of their life  and has it changed how you  approach stepping down as a  parliamentarian because presumably  your next 30 years are going to be  different now did you think about  carrying on working no i haven't changed  my mind about standing down i've been an  mp for for decades and for each day of  those decades i felt a great sense of  responsibility for my constituents for  my role in parliament but at the end of  the day you can't carry on indefinitely  and i want to go when i'm still fully  committed and doing the job to the best  of my ability and you know i'll see  what's on the other side of stepping  down from having those magic letters mp  after my name well i'm sure that you  will be doing many more interesting  things and i think your grandchildren  can be keeping you very busy anyway  harriet thanks so much for coming on the  show

